				\begin{document}$$l/d\approx 330$$\end{document} ) provides sufficient embedment to develop frictional pull-out resistance while avoiding systematic blockage during SCC flow through reinforcement. Fiber dispersion is equally critical. Uniformly distributed fibers enhance crack bridging, reduce pore connectivity, and improve the fiber–paste interfacial transition zone (ITZ), whereas poor dispersion can create porous, fiber-lean regions that weaken performance [ 85 ,  102 ]. Hydration products accumulating around nylon fibers further improve bond and post-cracking stress transfer [ 3 ]. Hybrid fiber systems offer additional benefits by combining mechanisms at different crack scales. Short microfibers control microcrack formation and shrinkage, while longer nylon or steel fibers bridge macro-cracks and sustain post-cracking load transfer [ 73 ,  108 ]. Such synergy has been shown to enhance toughness and ductility beyond single-fiber systems, including hybrid nylon–sisal SCC reported by Rahesh et al. [ 86 ]. In composite cement systems, the denser SCM-modified matrix may further strengthen fiber anchorage and improve this synergistic response.

